 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/De6Fw33gRGm/",
 "title": "Sales is not about forcing customers",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "Sales is not about pressuring customers to buy or constantly pushing discounts; instead, it is about understanding their exact needs and helping them make a purchase decision. A good salesperson asks the right questions instead of just talking more.",
 "key_points": [
  "Sales is not about convincing or pressuring someone to buy something they hesitate to get.",
  "If a customer says the price is high and wants to think about it, do not pressure them by saying the offer is only for today or that prices will go up.",
  "Understand the customer's actual needs by asking questions about their budget, capacity, and electricity or other usage costs.",
  "A good salesperson does not try to force every customer to buy; they help customers understand the product's value.",
  "Many business owners think sales means handling every objection like high prices or competitor discounts, but the key is understanding the customer first.",
  "Instead of talking a lot to push a sale, ask the right questions to help the customer make the right decision.",
  "The creator's core rule: Don't sell harder, sell better."
 ],
 "content": "Sales என்றால் கஸ்டமரை கன்வின்ஸ் செய்வது அல்ல #success #morningvibes #businessmindset #business #sales\n\n[unclear] We buy a lot of things in a month. A mobile, a shirt, things needed for the house, maybe even a small water bottle. But someone planned somewhere to sell that product to us, thought about how to bring it to us, and thought about how to make us buy it. Many products we buy happily. Even after buying, we think we bought a good product. Then we have to think about one thing: do we like selling or the way of selling? For example, if you sell things needed for a house, suppose a customer comes to your shop and looks at a washing machine. The moment he asks the price, he says it is a bit high and that he will think about it and come back. What will you do? Sir, this offer is only for today. You won't get a better product than this. Buy it now itself, the price will increase. You keep trying to make him buy. But what is the customer really thinking? Is this machine right for his budget? Does he have the necessary facilities? Is buying it at this price worth it? Before getting answers to all these questions, we try to make him buy. This is the difference between good sales and convincing or pressuring someone to sell. If a customer is hesitating to buy, do not just think about how to convince him somehow. First understand what his need is. Sir, what kind of washing machine are you looking at? Is budget important to you, capacity, or reducing electricity cost? If you ask such questions, you will start understanding what he needs. According to that, you can recommend the right product. If he is ready to buy, he will buy. If it is not the right product, he might walk away without buying. There is nothing wrong with that. But our job is not to somehow make every customer buy. Our job is to make people who need our product understand the value of our product. Many business owners think sales means knowing how to answer every objection from the customer. How to handle if price is high? How to convince if they say no? How to deal if competitors are giving more discount? But before all this comes, understand the customer properly, give them the necessary information, and make them ready to buy. Therefore, a good salesperson will not talk more and try to make the customer buy. He will ask the right questions and help the customer make the right decision. Remember one thing: to win in sales, you don't need to learn to force the customer more; you need to learn to sell better to them.\n\nText on screen:\n- Dont sell Harder\n- sell better",
